From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LosDaemons13 <esteveloris@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 14:44:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] rewriter: code mods, a mod's .pchtxt and .ips patches written
 before the rewrite
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Switch mods that change a game's code ship as byte patches keyed by the
module's build ID: IPSwitch text (.pchtxt) and IPS / IPS32 files in a mod's
exefs/ (yuzu, Ryujinx) or exefs_patches/<name>/ (Atmosphere). Horizon's loader
applies them before the game starts. The rewriter now does the same:
DM_EXEFS_MODS names mod folders, and patch_nso.py writes the patches each has
for its module's build into the image before anything reads the code, so the
sites, the function matchers and changed.tsv see the code the mod makes.
Patches into bss are placed with bss when the image carries it. Two mods that
write different bytes to one address, a write before or past the module and a
mod that changes bytes a registered cut needs stop the rewrite with a red
line. <module>.mods.tsv lists what was written.

The NSP script takes --mods <folder of mods> (MODS setting): it names, per mod,
the module it patches, a mod made for another version (its build ID is not
one of this version's modules) and an exlaunch mod (exefs/subsdk*,
main.npdm), which replaces modules and is not taken. The mods' patch files are
in the rewrite's reuse key only when there are mods, so builds without them
keep their key.

Checked on Pokémon Scarlet 4.0.0 (main 421c5411…): the 9 patches of a 60 fps
mod land on the instructions they name (add w1, w20, #1 -> mov w1, #1, the
present interval; mov w10, #1.0 -> #60.0).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: Code mods: a mod's .pchtxt and .ips patches go into the module before the rewrite, for the Windows launcher's Mods window
Issue: none

